add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "shoot_apical_meristem",
  `
<h2>Shoot Apical Meristem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem is a region of actively dividing cells near the tip of a growing shoot.</p>

<h3>What Does It Do?</h3>

<p>Cells produced by the shoot apical meristem contribute to primary growth of the shoot.</p>

<p>New cells can later elongate and differentiate into specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>Primary Growth</h3>

<p>Primary growth increases the length of a plant.</p>

<p>In shoots, the shoot apical meristem contributes to this increase in length.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>If cells in the shoot apical meristem divide and produce new cells, these cells can later enlarge and become specialised.</p>

<p>As this process continues, the shoot becomes longer and develops new tissues and leaves.</p>

<h3>Comparison with the Root</h3>

<p><strong>Root apical meristem → primary growth of the root.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Shoot apical meristem → primary growth of the shoot.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The meristem produces new cells; it does not mean that every cell at the shoot tip remains permanently undifferentiated.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Where is the shoot apical meristem found?",
      answer: "Near the tip of a growing shoot",
      why: "It is the main meristematic region responsible for shoot primary growth."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What type of growth does the shoot apical meristem contribute to?",
      answer: "Primary growth",
      why: "Primary growth increases the length of the shoot."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What happens to many cells produced by the shoot apical meristem?",
      options: [
        "They can elongate and differentiate into specialised tissues",
        "They immediately become mature seeds",
        "They all remain meristematic forever",
        "They lose all ability to carry out metabolism"
      ],
      answer: "They can elongate and differentiate into specialised tissues",
      why: "New cells develop into the tissues of the growing shoot."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "root_apical_meristem",
  `
<h2>Root Apical Meristem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem is a region of actively dividing cells near the tip of a growing root.</p>

<h3>Function</h3>

<p>Cells divide by mitosis, increasing the number of cells available for root growth.</p>

<p>New cells then move away from the meristem and can elongate and differentiate.</p>

<h3>Connection to Root Growth</h3>

<p>The root grows longer as new cells are produced and existing cells elongate.</p>

<p>Further from the tip, cells differentiate into specialised types including root hair cells and conducting tissues.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>If mitosis in the root apical meristem increases, more new cells can be produced.</p>

<p>If those cells subsequently elongate, the root can increase in length.</p>

<h3>Root Tip Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Root cap → cell division region → elongation region → differentiation region.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem should not be confused with the root cap.</p>

<p>The root cap protects the tip; the meristem produces new cells.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What process occurs actively in the root apical meristem?",
      answer: "Mitosis",
      why: "Meristematic cells divide to produce new cells."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why does cell division in the root apical meristem contribute to root growth?",
      answer: "It increases the number of cells available for elongation and differentiation",
      why: "New cells are needed to build additional root tissue."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which structure mainly protects the root tip rather than producing new cells?",
      options: [
        "Root cap",
        "Root apical meristem",
        "Zone of elongation",
        "Root hair"
      ],
      answer: "Root cap",
      why: "The root cap protects the delicate growing tip."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_processes_complete",
  `
<h2>Plant Growth Processes: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth involves several linked cellular processes rather than one single event.</p>

<h3>Stage 1: Cell Division</h3>

<p>Meristematic cells divide by mitosis.</p>

<p>This increases cell number.</p>

<h3>Stage 2: Cell Elongation</h3>

<p>Many newly produced cells increase in size and length.</p>

<p>Water uptake and expansion of the cell and vacuole contribute to this process.</p>

<h3>Stage 3: Cell Differentiation</h3>

<p>Cells develop specialised structures and functions.</p>

<p>Examples include root hair cells, xylem cells and phloem cells.</p>

<h3>Worked Exam Problem</h3>

<p>A student observes that the root of a seedling becomes longer over several days.</p>

<p>The student asks how cells contribute to this increase in length.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Cells in the root apical meristem divide by mitosis.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> New cells are produced.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Many of these cells elongate.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Cells differentiate into specialised tissues.</p>

<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Root growth results from coordinated cell division, cell elongation and differentiation.</p>

<h3>Critical Distinction</h3>

<p>Cell division increases the number of cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases cell size.</p>

<p>Differentiation produces specialised cells.</p>

<p>These processes work together to produce a functional growing plant.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Which process increases cell number during plant growth?",
      answer: "Cell division by mitosis",
      why: "Mitosis produces new daughter cells."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Which process increases the size or length of individual plant cells?",
      answer: "Cell elongation",
      why: "Elongation expands individual cells."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why is cell differentiation important after new cells are produced?",
      answer: "It allows cells to become specialised for different functions",
      why: "A functional plant requires different specialised tissues."
    }
  ]
);
add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_rate_calculation",
  `
<h2>Calculating Plant Growth Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can be measured by recording a change in length over a known period.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time taken</strong></p>

<p>First calculate the change:</p>

<p><strong>Change in length = final length − initial length</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>A root is initially 2 cm long and becomes 8 cm long after 3 days.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A shoot increases from 5 cm to 11 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Change:</p>

<p>11 − 5 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 cm/day</p>

<h3>Why Rate Is Useful</h3>

<p>Two plants may increase by the same amount but take different amounts of time.</p>

<p>The faster plant has the greater growth rate.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Always subtract the initial measurement before dividing by time.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A root grows from 3 cm to 9 cm in 3 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "(9 − 3) ÷ 3 = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A shoot grows from 4 cm to 10 cm in 2 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "3 cm/day",
      why: "(10 − 4) ÷ 2 = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A root increases from 2.5 cm to 8.5 cm in 4 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "1.5 cm/day",
      why: "(8.5 − 2.5) ÷ 4 = 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 cm/day."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_graph_gradient",
  `
<h2>Using the Gradient of a Growth Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A graph can show how plant length changes with time.</p>

<p>The gradient of a length-time graph represents the rate of growth.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Gradient = change in length ÷ change in time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is 2 cm long at Day 1 and 8 cm long at Day 4.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Change in time:</p>

<p>4 − 1 = 3 days</p>

<p>Gradient:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Interpreting the Graph</h3>

<p>A steep positive gradient means the plant is growing rapidly.</p>

<p>A smaller positive gradient means the plant is still growing but more slowly.</p>

<p>A horizontal section means there is no increase in the measured length during that interval.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The height of a point tells you the plant's measured length at that time.</p>

<p>The gradient tells you how quickly the length is changing.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What does the gradient of a length-time graph represent?",
      answer: "Growth rate",
      why: "Gradient is change in length divided by change in time."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A seedling increases from 3 cm at Day 2 to 9 cm at Day 5. Calculate the gradient.",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "(9 − 3) ÷ (5 − 2) = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What does a horizontal section of a length-time graph indicate?",
      options: [
        "No increase in the measured length during that interval",
        "Maximum growth rate",
        "The plant has doubled in length",
        "The plant has disappeared"
      ],
      answer: "No increase in the measured length during that interval",
      why: "A horizontal line has zero gradient."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_graph_stages",
  `
<h2>Identifying Stages of Growth from a Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A plant growth graph can show periods when growth is fast, slow or temporarily unchanged.</p>

<h3>Fast Growth</h3>

<p>A steep gradient indicates a high growth rate.</p>

<h3>Slow Growth</h3>

<p>A less steep gradient indicates a lower growth rate.</p>

<h3>No Measured Growth</h3>

<p>A horizontal section indicates that the measured variable has not increased during that period.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Suppose a shoot grows:</p>

<p>Day 0–2: 1 cm → 3 cm</p>

<p>Day 2–4: 3 cm → 7 cm</p>

<p>Day 4–6: 7 cm → 8 cm</p>

<p>Calculate each rate.</p>

<p>Day 0–2:</p>

<p>(3 − 1) ÷ 2 = 1 cm/day</p>

<p>Day 2–4:</p>

<p>(7 − 3) ÷ 2 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>Day 4–6:</p>

<p>(8 − 7) ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm/day</p>

<p>The plant therefore grew fastest between Days 2 and 4.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p>Never judge growth rate only from the final height.</p>

<p>Compare the gradients of the relevant sections.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A plant grows from 1 cm to 3 cm between Day 0 and Day 2. What is the growth rate?",
      answer: "1 cm/day",
      why: "(3 − 1) ÷ 2 = 1 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A plant grows from 3 cm to 7 cm between Day 2 and Day 4. What is the growth rate?",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "(7 − 3) ÷ 2 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "During which interval was growth fastest in the example?",
      options: [
        "Day 0–2",
        "Day 2–4",
        "Day 4–6",
        "All intervals were equal"
      ],
      answer: "Day 2–4",
      why: "The growth rate was 2 cm/day, the highest of the three intervals."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "comparing_plant_growth_conditions",
  `
<h2>Comparing Plant Growth Under Different Conditions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Scientists can compare plant growth under different environmental conditions by changing one factor and measuring growth.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of seedlings are grown under different light intensities.</p>

<p>Group A receives high light intensity.</p>

<p>Group B receives low light intensity.</p>

<p>Other important conditions are kept similar.</p>

<h3>What Is Measured?</h3>

<p>Possible dependent variables include:</p>

<p>• increase in height</p>

<p>• root length</p>

<p>• dry mass</p>

<p>• growth rate</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Group A increases by 8 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Group B increases by 4 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>A growth rate:</p>

<p>8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>B growth rate:</p>

<p>4 ÷ 4 = 1 cm/day</p>

<p>Group A had the greater measured growth rate.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>A difference in growth does not automatically prove that the tested factor caused the difference unless other important variables were controlled.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Group A grows 8 cm in 4 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Group B grows 4 cm in 4 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "1 cm/day",
      why: "4 ÷ 4 = 1 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Why should important variables other than the tested factor be controlled?",
      options: [
        "To make the comparison fair",
        "To make the seedlings identical genetically",
        "To prevent all growth",
        "To remove the need for measurements"
      ],
      answer: "To make the comparison fair",
      why: "Uncontrolled variables could provide alternative explanations for differences in growth."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_experiment_variables",
  `
<h2>Variables in a Plant Growth Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A well-designed plant growth experiment requires clear identification of variables.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The independent variable is the factor deliberately changed by the investigator.</p>

<p>Example: light intensity.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The dependent variable is what is measured.</p>

<p>Example: growth rate in cm/day.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>These are factors kept constant so that they do not provide alternative explanations for the result.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>• plant species</p>

<p>• starting size</p>

<p>• water supply</p>

<p>• mineral supply</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• duration</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates how temperature affects seedling growth.</p>

<p><strong>Independent:</strong> temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent:</strong> growth rate.</p>

<p><strong>Controls:</strong> plant species, starting size, water, light, minerals and duration.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>"Growth rate" is not the independent variable if the student is measuring it.</p>

<p>It is the dependent variable.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "A student changes temperature and measures shoot growth rate. What is the independent variable?",
      options: [
        "Temperature",
        "Shoot growth rate",
        "Plant species",
        "Amount of growth recorded"
      ],
      answer: "Temperature",
      why: "Temperature is the factor deliberately changed."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What is the dependent variable in this investigation?",
      answer: "Shoot growth rate",
      why: "Growth rate is the measurement being recorded."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Give two variables that could be controlled.",
      answer: "Plant species and water supply",
      why: "Keeping these constant helps make the temperature comparison fair."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_experiment_repeats",
  `
<h2>Repeating a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth varies naturally between individual plants.</p>

<p>Repeating measurements helps determine whether an observed pattern is consistent.</p>

<h3>Using Replicates</h3>

<p>Instead of using one seedling per treatment, several seedlings can be used.</p>

<p>The growth of each seedling can be measured.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three seedlings under one condition grow:</p>

<p>4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm.</p>

<p>Mean growth:</p>

<p>(4 + 5 + 6) ÷ 3 = 15 ÷ 3 = 5 cm</p>

<h3>Why the Mean Helps</h3>

<p>The mean gives a summary of the measurements rather than relying on one individual plant.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>If repeated groups produce similar results, confidence in the consistency of the pattern increases.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Repeating an experiment does not remove systematic errors.</p>

<p>A consistently biased method can still produce consistent but inaccurate results.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should several seedlings be used instead of only one?",
      answer: "To reduce the influence of natural differences between individual seedlings",
      why: "Individual plants can vary in growth."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Calculate the mean growth for seedlings that grew 4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm.",
      answer: "5 cm",
      why: "(4 + 5 + 6) ÷ 3 = 5 cm."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What does repeating an experiment mainly help assess?",
      options: [
        "Reliability",
        "The colour of the seedlings",
        "The species classification",
        "Whether plants contain DNA"
      ],
      answer: "Reliability",
      why: "Repeats show whether similar results are obtained consistently."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_anomalous_result",
  `
<h2>Anomalous Results in a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An anomalous result is a measurement that does not fit the general pattern of the other results.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Five seedlings under the same conditions grow:</p>

<p>5 cm, 5.2 cm, 4.8 cm, 5.1 cm and 9.5 cm.</p>

<p>The 9.5 cm result is much higher than the others and may be anomalous.</p>

<h3>What Should the Student Do?</h3>

<p>The result should not simply be deleted because it is inconvenient.</p>

<p>The student should investigate possible causes.</p>

<h3>Possible Causes</h3>

<p>• measurement error</p>

<p>• damaged equipment</p>

<p>• a recording mistake</p>

<p>• an unusual biological difference</p>

<p>• an uncontrolled environmental difference</p>

<h3>Repeating the Measurement</h3>

<p>If possible, repeat the measurement or investigate the original plant.</p>

<p>The reason for the unusual result should be considered before deciding how to handle it.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>An anomalous result is not automatically an error.</p>

<p>It may represent genuine biological variation.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which value is most likely anomalous in the example?",
      options: [
        "5 cm",
        "5.2 cm",
        "4.8 cm",
        "9.5 cm"
      ],
      answer: "9.5 cm",
      why: "It is substantially different from the other measurements."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What should a student do before simply removing an anomalous result?",
      answer: "Investigate or repeat the measurement if possible",
      why: "The unusual result may be due to an error or genuine biological variation."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should an anomalous result not automatically be discarded?",
      answer: "It may represent genuine biological variation",
      why: "Not every unusual result is caused by experimental error."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_data_conclusion",
  `
<h2>Drawing a Conclusion from Plant Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A scientific conclusion should be based directly on the evidence collected.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates temperature and seedling growth.</p>

<p>Mean growth rates are:</p>

<p>15°C → 0.8 cm/day</p>

<p>25°C → 1.6 cm/day</p>

<p>35°C → 1.2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Pattern</h3>

<p>Growth rate increases from 15°C to 25°C.</p>

<p>Growth rate then decreases at 35°C.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Highest Tested Value</h3>

<p>25°C gives the highest measured growth rate.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Write the Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, seedling growth rate was highest at 25°C.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Avoid Overclaiming</h3>

<p>It would be too strong to say that 25°C is the optimum temperature for all plants.</p>

<p>The evidence only supports the tested species and experimental conditions.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p><strong>State the pattern → quote relevant data → give a limited conclusion.</strong></p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "At which tested temperature was growth rate highest?",
      answer: "25°C",
      why: "The growth rate was 1.6 cm/day, the highest recorded value."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What happened to growth rate between 25°C and 35°C?",
      answer: "It decreased from 1.6 cm/day to 1.2 cm/day",
      why: "The data show a decrease of 0.4 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which conclusion is scientifically appropriate?",
      options: [
        "25°C produced the highest measured growth rate under the conditions tested",
        "25°C is the optimum temperature for every plant",
        "Temperature has no effect on growth",
        "All plants grow fastest at 25°C"
      ],
      answer: "25°C produced the highest measured growth rate under the conditions tested",
      why: "A conclusion should not extend beyond the evidence."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_investigation_evaluation",
  `
<h2>Evaluating a Plant Growth Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>After collecting results, an investigation can be evaluated by considering its reliability, validity and possible improvements.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>Use several plants and repeat measurements.</p>

<p>Calculate means where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Validity</h3>

<p>Change only the factor being investigated.</p>

<p>Keep other important conditions controlled.</p>

<h3>Accuracy</h3>

<p>Use suitable measuring equipment and read measurements consistently.</p>

<h3>Improvement Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates light intensity using one seedling in each group.</p>

<p>This is weak because natural differences between individual seedlings could affect the result.</p>

<p>A better design uses several similar seedlings per treatment and calculates mean growth.</p>

<h3>Another Improvement</h3>

<p>If temperature changes between groups while light intensity also changes, the investigation cannot confidently attribute growth differences to temperature.</p>

<p>Light intensity should therefore be controlled.</p>

<h3>Final Principle</h3>

<p>A strong investigation reduces alternative explanations for the observed result.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "How can using several seedlings improve an investigation?",
      answer: "It reduces the influence of natural differences between individual plants and improves reliability",
      why: "More observations provide stronger evidence."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should light intensity be controlled when investigating temperature?",
      answer: "Because light intensity can also affect plant growth",
      why: "Otherwise light could become an alternative explanation for differences in growth."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which design is strongest?",
      options: [
        "Several similar seedlings, one factor changed, other important factors controlled, repeated measurements",
        "One seedling with several factors changed at once",
        "Different plant species with different amounts of water and light",
        "One measurement with no controls"
      ],
      answer: "Several similar seedlings, one factor changed, other important factors controlled, repeated measurements",
      why: "This design improves the fairness and reliability of the investigation."
    }
  ]
);
add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_rate_calculation",
  `
<h2>Calculating Plant Growth Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can be measured by recording a change in length over a known period.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time taken</strong></p>

<p>First calculate the change:</p>

<p><strong>Change in length = final length − initial length</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>A root is initially 2 cm long and becomes 8 cm long after 3 days.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A shoot increases from 5 cm to 11 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Change:</p>

<p>11 − 5 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 cm/day</p>

<h3>Why Rate Is Useful</h3>

<p>Two plants may increase by the same amount but take different amounts of time.</p>

<p>The faster plant has the greater growth rate.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Always subtract the initial measurement before dividing by time.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A root grows from 3 cm to 9 cm in 3 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "(9 − 3) ÷ 3 = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A shoot grows from 4 cm to 10 cm in 2 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "3 cm/day",
      why: "(10 − 4) ÷ 2 = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A root increases from 2.5 cm to 8.5 cm in 4 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "1.5 cm/day",
      why: "(8.5 − 2.5) ÷ 4 = 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 cm/day."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_graph_gradient",
  `
<h2>Using the Gradient of a Growth Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A graph can show how plant length changes with time.</p>

<p>The gradient of a length-time graph represents the rate of growth.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Gradient = change in length ÷ change in time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is 2 cm long at Day 1 and 8 cm long at Day 4.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Change in time:</p>

<p>4 − 1 = 3 days</p>

<p>Gradient:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Interpreting the Graph</h3>

<p>A steep positive gradient means the plant is growing rapidly.</p>

<p>A smaller positive gradient means the plant is still growing but more slowly.</p>

<p>A horizontal section means there is no increase in the measured length during that interval.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The height of a point tells you the plant's measured length at that time.</p>

<p>The gradient tells you how quickly the length is changing.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What does the gradient of a length-time graph represent?",
      answer: "Growth rate",
      why: "Gradient is change in length divided by change in time."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A seedling increases from 3 cm at Day 2 to 9 cm at Day 5. Calculate the gradient.",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "(9 − 3) ÷ (5 − 2) = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What does a horizontal section of a length-time graph indicate?",
      options: [
        "No increase in the measured length during that interval",
        "Maximum growth rate",
        "The plant has doubled in length",
        "The plant has disappeared"
      ],
      answer: "No increase in the measured length during that interval",
      why: "A horizontal line has zero gradient."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_graph_stages",
  `
<h2>Identifying Stages of Growth from a Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A plant growth graph can show periods when growth is fast, slow or temporarily unchanged.</p>

<h3>Fast Growth</h3>

<p>A steep gradient indicates a high growth rate.</p>

<h3>Slow Growth</h3>

<p>A less steep gradient indicates a lower growth rate.</p>

<h3>No Measured Growth</h3>

<p>A horizontal section indicates that the measured variable has not increased during that period.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Suppose a shoot grows:</p>

<p>Day 0–2: 1 cm → 3 cm</p>

<p>Day 2–4: 3 cm → 7 cm</p>

<p>Day 4–6: 7 cm → 8 cm</p>

<p>Calculate each rate.</p>

<p>Day 0–2:</p>

<p>(3 − 1) ÷ 2 = 1 cm/day</p>

<p>Day 2–4:</p>

<p>(7 − 3) ÷ 2 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>Day 4–6:</p>

<p>(8 − 7) ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm/day</p>

<p>The plant therefore grew fastest between Days 2 and 4.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p>Never judge growth rate only from the final height.</p>

<p>Compare the gradients of the relevant sections.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A plant grows from 1 cm to 3 cm between Day 0 and Day 2. What is the growth rate?",
      answer: "1 cm/day",
      why: "(3 − 1) ÷ 2 = 1 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A plant grows from 3 cm to 7 cm between Day 2 and Day 4. What is the growth rate?",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "(7 − 3) ÷ 2 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "During which interval was growth fastest in the example?",
      options: [
        "Day 0–2",
        "Day 2–4",
        "Day 4–6",
        "All intervals were equal"
      ],
      answer: "Day 2–4",
      why: "The growth rate was 2 cm/day, the highest of the three intervals."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "comparing_plant_growth_conditions",
  `
<h2>Comparing Plant Growth Under Different Conditions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Scientists can compare plant growth under different environmental conditions by changing one factor and measuring growth.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of seedlings are grown under different light intensities.</p>

<p>Group A receives high light intensity.</p>

<p>Group B receives low light intensity.</p>

<p>Other important conditions are kept similar.</p>

<h3>What Is Measured?</h3>

<p>Possible dependent variables include:</p>

<p>• increase in height</p>

<p>• root length</p>

<p>• dry mass</p>

<p>• growth rate</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Group A increases by 8 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Group B increases by 4 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>A growth rate:</p>

<p>8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>B growth rate:</p>

<p>4 ÷ 4 = 1 cm/day</p>

<p>Group A had the greater measured growth rate.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>A difference in growth does not automatically prove that the tested factor caused the difference unless other important variables were controlled.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Group A grows 8 cm in 4 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "2 cm/day",
      why: "8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Group B grows 4 cm in 4 days. Calculate its growth rate.",
      answer: "1 cm/day",
      why: "4 ÷ 4 = 1 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Why should important variables other than the tested factor be controlled?",
      options: [
        "To make the comparison fair",
        "To make the seedlings identical genetically",
        "To prevent all growth",
        "To remove the need for measurements"
      ],
      answer: "To make the comparison fair",
      why: "Uncontrolled variables could provide alternative explanations for differences in growth."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_experiment_variables",
  `
<h2>Variables in a Plant Growth Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A well-designed plant growth experiment requires clear identification of variables.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The independent variable is the factor deliberately changed by the investigator.</p>

<p>Example: light intensity.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The dependent variable is what is measured.</p>

<p>Example: growth rate in cm/day.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>These are factors kept constant so that they do not provide alternative explanations for the result.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>• plant species</p>

<p>• starting size</p>

<p>• water supply</p>

<p>• mineral supply</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• duration</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates how temperature affects seedling growth.</p>

<p><strong>Independent:</strong> temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent:</strong> growth rate.</p>

<p><strong>Controls:</strong> plant species, starting size, water, light, minerals and duration.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>"Growth rate" is not the independent variable if the student is measuring it.</p>

<p>It is the dependent variable.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "A student changes temperature and measures shoot growth rate. What is the independent variable?",
      options: [
        "Temperature",
        "Shoot growth rate",
        "Plant species",
        "Amount of growth recorded"
      ],
      answer: "Temperature",
      why: "Temperature is the factor deliberately changed."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What is the dependent variable in this investigation?",
      answer: "Shoot growth rate",
      why: "Growth rate is the measurement being recorded."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Give two variables that could be controlled.",
      answer: "Plant species and water supply",
      why: "Keeping these constant helps make the temperature comparison fair."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_experiment_repeats",
  `
<h2>Repeating a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth varies naturally between individual plants.</p>

<p>Repeating measurements helps determine whether an observed pattern is consistent.</p>

<h3>Using Replicates</h3>

<p>Instead of using one seedling per treatment, several seedlings can be used.</p>

<p>The growth of each seedling can be measured.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three seedlings under one condition grow:</p>

<p>4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm.</p>

<p>Mean growth:</p>

<p>(4 + 5 + 6) ÷ 3 = 15 ÷ 3 = 5 cm</p>

<h3>Why the Mean Helps</h3>

<p>The mean gives a summary of the measurements rather than relying on one individual plant.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>If repeated groups produce similar results, confidence in the consistency of the pattern increases.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Repeating an experiment does not remove systematic errors.</p>

<p>A consistently biased method can still produce consistent but inaccurate results.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should several seedlings be used instead of only one?",
      answer: "To reduce the influence of natural differences between individual seedlings",
      why: "Individual plants can vary in growth."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Calculate the mean growth for seedlings that grew 4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm.",
      answer: "5 cm",
      why: "(4 + 5 + 6) ÷ 3 = 5 cm."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What does repeating an experiment mainly help assess?",
      options: [
        "Reliability",
        "The colour of the seedlings",
        "The species classification",
        "Whether plants contain DNA"
      ],
      answer: "Reliability",
      why: "Repeats show whether similar results are obtained consistently."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_anomalous_result",
  `
<h2>Anomalous Results in a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An anomalous result is a measurement that does not fit the general pattern of the other results.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Five seedlings under the same conditions grow:</p>

<p>5 cm, 5.2 cm, 4.8 cm, 5.1 cm and 9.5 cm.</p>

<p>The 9.5 cm result is much higher than the others and may be anomalous.</p>

<h3>What Should the Student Do?</h3>

<p>The result should not simply be deleted because it is inconvenient.</p>

<p>The student should investigate possible causes.</p>

<h3>Possible Causes</h3>

<p>• measurement error</p>

<p>• damaged equipment</p>

<p>• a recording mistake</p>

<p>• an unusual biological difference</p>

<p>• an uncontrolled environmental difference</p>

<h3>Repeating the Measurement</h3>

<p>If possible, repeat the measurement or investigate the original plant.</p>

<p>The reason for the unusual result should be considered before deciding how to handle it.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>An anomalous result is not automatically an error.</p>

<p>It may represent genuine biological variation.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which value is most likely anomalous in the example?",
      options: [
        "5 cm",
        "5.2 cm",
        "4.8 cm",
        "9.5 cm"
      ],
      answer: "9.5 cm",
      why: "It is substantially different from the other measurements."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What should a student do before simply removing an anomalous result?",
      answer: "Investigate or repeat the measurement if possible",
      why: "The unusual result may be due to an error or genuine biological variation."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should an anomalous result not automatically be discarded?",
      answer: "It may represent genuine biological variation",
      why: "Not every unusual result is caused by experimental error."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_data_conclusion",
  `
<h2>Drawing a Conclusion from Plant Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A scientific conclusion should be based directly on the evidence collected.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates temperature and seedling growth.</p>

<p>Mean growth rates are:</p>

<p>15°C → 0.8 cm/day</p>

<p>25°C → 1.6 cm/day</p>

<p>35°C → 1.2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Pattern</h3>

<p>Growth rate increases from 15°C to 25°C.</p>

<p>Growth rate then decreases at 35°C.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Highest Tested Value</h3>

<p>25°C gives the highest measured growth rate.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Write the Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, seedling growth rate was highest at 25°C.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Avoid Overclaiming</h3>

<p>It would be too strong to say that 25°C is the optimum temperature for all plants.</p>

<p>The evidence only supports the tested species and experimental conditions.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p><strong>State the pattern → quote relevant data → give a limited conclusion.</strong></p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "At which tested temperature was growth rate highest?",
      answer: "25°C",
      why: "The growth rate was 1.6 cm/day, the highest recorded value."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What happened to growth rate between 25°C and 35°C?",
      answer: "It decreased from 1.6 cm/day to 1.2 cm/day",
      why: "The data show a decrease of 0.4 cm/day."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which conclusion is scientifically appropriate?",
      options: [
        "25°C produced the highest measured growth rate under the conditions tested",
        "25°C is the optimum temperature for every plant",
        "Temperature has no effect on growth",
        "All plants grow fastest at 25°C"
      ],
      answer: "25°C produced the highest measured growth rate under the conditions tested",
      why: "A conclusion should not extend beyond the evidence."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "plant_growth_investigation_evaluation",
  `
<h2>Evaluating a Plant Growth Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>After collecting results, an investigation can be evaluated by considering its reliability, validity and possible improvements.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>Use several plants and repeat measurements.</p>

<p>Calculate means where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Validity</h3>

<p>Change only the factor being investigated.</p>

<p>Keep other important conditions controlled.</p>

<h3>Accuracy</h3>

<p>Use suitable measuring equipment and read measurements consistently.</p>

<h3>Improvement Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates light intensity using one seedling in each group.</p>

<p>This is weak because natural differences between individual seedlings could affect the result.</p>

<p>A better design uses several similar seedlings per treatment and calculates mean growth.</p>

<h3>Another Improvement</h3>

<p>If temperature changes between groups while light intensity also changes, the investigation cannot confidently attribute growth differences to temperature.</p>

<p>Light intensity should therefore be controlled.</p>

<h3>Final Principle</h3>

<p>A strong investigation reduces alternative explanations for the observed result.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "How can using several seedlings improve an investigation?",
      answer: "It reduces the influence of natural differences between individual plants and improves reliability",
      why: "More observations provide stronger evidence."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should light intensity be controlled when investigating temperature?",
      answer: "Because light intensity can also affect plant growth",
      why: "Otherwise light could become an alternative explanation for differences in growth."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which design is strongest?",
      options: [
        "Several similar seedlings, one factor changed, other important factors controlled, repeated measurements",
        "One seedling with several factors changed at once",
        "Different plant species with different amounts of water and light",
        "One measurement with no controls"
      ],
      answer: "Several similar seedlings, one factor changed, other important factors controlled, repeated measurements",
      why: "This design improves the fairness and reliability of the investigation."
    }
  ]
);
add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "secondary_growth_lateral_meristems",
  `
<h2>Secondary Growth and Lateral Meristems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is the increase in the thickness or girth of a plant stem or root.</p>

<p>It differs from primary growth, which mainly increases the length of the plant.</p>

<h3>Where Does Secondary Growth Occur?</h3>

<p>Secondary growth occurs through the activity of <strong>lateral meristems</strong>.</p>

<p>Lateral meristems contain cells that can divide by mitosis and produce new cells.</p>

<p>The main lateral meristems involved are the <strong>vascular cambium</strong> and <strong>cork cambium</strong>.</p>

<h3>Vascular Cambium</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces new vascular tissues.</p>

<p>It produces secondary xylem towards the inside of the stem and secondary phloem towards the outside.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Increase Girth?</h3>

<p>As new cells are produced and added to the stem, the stem becomes wider.</p>

<h3>Primary vs Secondary Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Primary growth:</strong> increase in length.</p>

<p><strong>Secondary growth:</strong> increase in thickness or girth.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>If a question asks how a woody stem becomes thicker, think of <strong>lateral meristems and secondary growth</strong>, not the shoot apical meristem.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What is secondary growth?",
      options: [
        "Increase in plant thickness or girth",
        "Increase in plant length only",
        "Production of gametes",
        "Movement of water through xylem"
      ],
      answer: "Increase in plant thickness or girth",
      why: "Secondary growth increases the diameter or girth of stems and roots."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Which type of meristem is responsible for secondary growth?",
      answer: "Lateral meristem",
      why: "Lateral meristems produce cells that increase the thickness of stems and roots."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "State one difference between primary growth and secondary growth.",
      answer: "Primary growth increases length while secondary growth increases thickness or girth",
      why: "The two types of growth occur in different directions and involve different meristematic regions."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "vascular_cambium_secondary_xylem_phloem",
  `
<h2>Vascular Cambium and the Production of Vascular Tissue</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>vascular cambium</strong> is a layer of dividing cells involved in secondary growth.</p>

<p>It produces new vascular tissue on both sides of the cambium.</p>

<h3>Towards the Inside</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces <strong>secondary xylem</strong> towards the centre of the stem.</p>

<p>Secondary xylem contributes to the woody part of the stem and helps transport water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Towards the Outside</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces <strong>secondary phloem</strong> towards the outside of the stem.</p>

<p>Phloem transports assimilates such as sucrose.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning</h3>

<p>A woody stem becomes wider as the vascular cambium continues producing new cells.</p>

<p>More secondary xylem is added inside the cambium and secondary phloem is added outside it.</p>

<p>Repeated production of these tissues contributes to an increase in stem girth.</p>

<h3>Key Direction</h3>

<p><strong>Vascular cambium → secondary xylem inward + secondary phloem outward.</strong></p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What tissue does the vascular cambium produce towards the inside of a stem?",
      options: [
        "Secondary xylem",
        "Secondary phloem",
        "Epidermis",
        "Root hair"
      ],
      answer: "Secondary xylem",
      why: "Secondary xylem is produced on the inner side of the vascular cambium."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What tissue does the vascular cambium produce towards the outside?",
      options: [
        "Secondary phloem",
        "Secondary xylem",
        "Root cap",
        "Palisade mesophyll"
      ],
      answer: "Secondary phloem",
      why: "Secondary phloem is produced on the outer side of the vascular cambium."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Explain how vascular cambium activity contributes to an increase in stem girth.",
      answer: "It divides and produces secondary xylem inward and secondary phloem outward, adding new tissue and increasing stem thickness",
      why: "Continued production of these tissues adds material to the stem."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "secondary_growth_woody_stems",
  `
<h2>Secondary Growth in Woody Stems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Woody plants can continue increasing in stem thickness after primary growth has increased the length of the stem.</p>

<p>This thickening is mainly associated with secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step-by-Step Process</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Lateral meristems remain capable of cell division.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The vascular cambium produces new secondary xylem and secondary phloem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Repeated production adds tissue to the stem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The diameter or girth of the stem increases.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Important?</h3>

<p>A thicker woody stem provides structural support as the plant becomes larger.</p>

<p>The additional vascular tissue also contributes to the plant's transport capacity.</p>

<h3>Exam Application</h3>

<p>If an older tree has a much thicker trunk than a young stem, this difference is not explained simply by elongation.</p>

<p>The increase in trunk diameter results largely from secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem is mainly responsible for primary growth.</p>

<p>Lateral meristems are responsible for secondary growth.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "What type of growth makes a woody stem become thicker?",
      answer: "Secondary growth",
      why: "Secondary growth increases the thickness or girth of stems."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Which meristem produces new vascular tissue during secondary growth?",
      answer: "Vascular cambium",
      why: "The vascular cambium divides and produces secondary xylem and secondary phloem."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which structure is mainly associated with primary growth rather than secondary growth?",
      options: [
        "Shoot apical meristem",
        "Vascular cambium",
        "Lateral meristem",
        "Cork cambium"
      ],
      answer: "Shoot apical meristem",
      why: "The shoot apical meristem produces cells that contribute mainly to lengthening of the shoot."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "annual_growth_rings",
  `
<h2>Annual Growth Rings</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In many woody plants, changes in growth conditions during different parts of the year can produce visible differences in the wood.</p>

<p>These differences can form growth rings.</p>

<h3>How Do Rings Form?</h3>

<p>When conditions favour rapid growth, the vascular cambium can produce wood with characteristics different from wood produced during slower growth.</p>

<p>Repeated changes in growth rate can therefore produce alternating bands in the wood.</p>

<h3>Reading a Growth Ring</h3>

<p>A sequence of rings can provide evidence about past patterns of growth.</p>

<p>However, a ring should not automatically be interpreted as proof of exactly one calendar year because growth patterns depend on the plant species and environmental conditions.</p>

<h3>Environmental Conditions</h3>

<p>Growth can be affected by factors such as:</p>

<p>• water availability</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• nutrient availability</p>

<p>• damage</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>If one period produced a narrow ring while another produced a wider ring, the difference may indicate different growth conditions.</p>

<p>The ring width alone does not identify the exact cause.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What can differences in growth rings provide evidence about?",
      options: [
        "Past patterns of plant growth",
        "The exact DNA sequence of the plant",
        "The number of leaves currently present",
        "The number of chromosomes in the plant"
      ],
      answer: "Past patterns of plant growth",
      why: "Differences in wood produced during different growth conditions can be preserved as rings."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Name two environmental factors that can affect plant growth and therefore potentially influence growth-ring patterns.",
      answer: "Water availability and temperature",
      why: "Environmental conditions influence the rate of plant growth."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why should a narrow growth ring not automatically be described as proof of drought?",
      answer: "Other factors can also reduce growth",
      why: "Temperature, nutrients, damage and other conditions may affect growth."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "secondary_growth_roots",
  `
<h2>Secondary Growth in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Some roots, particularly roots of woody plants, also undergo secondary growth.</p>

<p>This increases the thickness or girth of the root.</p>

<h3>Primary Root Growth</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem contributes mainly to increase in root length.</p>

<h3>Secondary Root Growth</h3>

<p>Lateral meristems contribute to increase in root thickness.</p>

<p>The vascular cambium produces additional vascular tissue as secondary growth continues.</p>

<h3>Worked Comparison</h3>

<p>A young root extending deeper into soil is showing primary growth.</p>

<p>An older woody root becoming thicker is showing secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Why Thickness Matters</h3>

<p>As a plant becomes larger, thicker roots can provide stronger support and contain additional vascular tissue.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Root apical meristem → mainly length.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Lateral meristems → thickness.</strong></p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "What does secondary growth increase in a woody root?",
      options: [
        "Thickness",
        "Length only",
        "Number of seeds",
        "Leaf area only"
      ],
      answer: "Thickness",
      why: "Secondary growth increases the girth of roots."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Which meristem is mainly responsible for increasing root length?",
      answer: "Root apical meristem",
      why: "Cells produced near the root tip contribute to primary growth."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A mature woody root becomes wider over time. Which type of growth explains this?",
      answer: "Secondary growth",
      why: "An increase in root thickness is secondary growth."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "primary_secondary_growth_data",
  `
<h2>Interpreting Primary and Secondary Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Growth measurements can be used to distinguish changes in length from changes in thickness.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A young stem has:</p>

<p>Initial length = 12 cm</p>

<p>Final length = 20 cm</p>

<p>Initial diameter = 0.5 cm</p>

<p>Final diameter = 0.7 cm</p>

<h3>Change in Length</h3>

<p>20 − 12 = 8 cm</p>

<p>The increase in length is evidence of primary growth.</p>

<h3>Change in Diameter</h3>

<p>0.7 − 0.5 = 0.2 cm</p>

<p>The increase in diameter is evidence of thickening and is associated with secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>A plant can undergo primary and secondary growth at the same time in different regions.</p>

<p>Therefore, primary and secondary growth are not necessarily mutually exclusive.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p>When analysing data, first identify <strong>what was measured</strong>.</p>

<p>Length → primary growth.</p>

<p>Thickness or diameter → secondary growth.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A stem increases from 12 cm to 20 cm in length. Calculate the increase in length.",
      answer: "8 cm",
      why: "20 − 12 = 8 cm."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "A stem diameter increases from 0.5 cm to 0.7 cm. Calculate the increase in diameter.",
      answer: "0.2 cm",
      why: "0.7 − 0.5 = 0.2 cm."
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which measurement would provide evidence of secondary growth?",
      options: [
        "Increase in stem diameter",
        "Increase in shoot length",
        "Increase in root length",
        "Increase in leaf number"
      ],
      answer: "Increase in stem diameter",
      why: "Secondary growth refers to an increase in thickness or girth."
    }
  ]
);


add(
  "biology",
  "plant_growth_and_development",
  "secondary_growth_environmental_effects",
  `
<h2>Environmental Factors and Secondary Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth depends on active cell division and the production of new tissues.</p>

<p>Environmental conditions can therefore affect the rate at which secondary growth occurs.</p>

<h3>Water Availability</h3>

<p>Water is required for normal plant metabolism and cell expansion.</p>

<p>Severe water shortage can reduce plant growth.</p>

<h3>Mineral Availability</h3>

<p>Plants require mineral ions to make important substances and maintain normal growth.</p>

<p>Severe mineral deficiency can therefore limit growth.</p>

<h3>Temperature</h3>

<p>Temperature affects enzyme-controlled metabolic processes.</p>

<p>Very low temperatures can slow metabolic reactions, while excessively high temperatures can damage cells and enzymes.</p>

<h3>Interpreting Evidence</h3>

<p>If two groups of woody plants show different increases in stem diameter, the difference may be related to environmental conditions if those conditions were experimentally controlled and compared.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not claim that one environmental factor caused the difference unless the investigation controlled other important variables.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      type: "mcq",
      q: "Which factor can affect plant growth and therefore potentially affect secondary growth?",
      options: [
        "Water availability",
        "Only the number of chromosomes",
        "Scientific name",
        "Leaf colour alone"
      ],
      answer: "Water availability",
      why: "Water is required for normal plant growth and metabolism."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why can severe mineral deficiency reduce plant growth?",
      answer: "Mineral ions are required to make important substances and support normal plant processes",
      why: "Deficiencies can limit processes required for growth."
    },
    {
      type: "short",
      q: "Why must other important conditions be controlled when testing the effect of temperature on secondary growth?",
      answer: "To prevent other factors from explaining differences in growth",
      why: "Controlled variables make the investigation a fair test of temperature."
    }
  ]
);


